  "stable_key": "g4-lesson-03",
  "position": 3,
  "title": "Sine, cosine and tangent in right-angled triangles",
  "summary": "Choose and apply sine, cosine or tangent to calculate unknown sides and acute angles, including contextual elevation problems.",
  "outcomes": [
    "4.3"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g4-lesson-01",
    "g4-lesson-02"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 70,
  "objectives": [
    "Label opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse relative to a chosen angle.",
    "Choose a trigonometric ratio containing the known and required quantities.",
    "Calculate sides and angles using degree mode and appropriate rounding."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-label",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Label sides relative to the angle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle. The opposite and adjacent labels depend on the acute reference angle θ. The adjacent side touches θ but is not the hypotenuse."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g4-lesson-03-trigonometry"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-ratios",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Choose a ratio",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse, cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse, and tangent uses opposite and adjacent. Choose the ratio containing the side you know and the quantity you need."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\sin\\theta=\\frac{O}{H},\\quad\\cos\\theta=\\frac{A}{H},\\quad\\tan\\theta=\\frac{O}{A}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-example-side",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: calculate a side",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A ramp makes a 28° angle with horizontal ground and has horizontal length 7.5 m. Find its vertical rise correct to 2 decimal places."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The rise is opposite and 7.5 m is adjacent."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\tan28^\\circ=\\frac{h}{7.5}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "h=7.5\\tan28^\\circ=3.99\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The rise is shorter than the horizontal run because the angle is less than 45°."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-example-hypotenuse",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: use sine when the hypotenuse is given",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 6 m ladder leans against a vertical wall and makes an angle of 70° with the horizontal ground. Find the height the ladder reaches up the wall, correct to 2 decimal places."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g4-lesson-03-sine"
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The ladder is the hypotenuse, 6 m. The height up the wall is opposite the 70° angle. Opposite and hypotenuse are in the sine ratio."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\sin70^\\circ=\\frac{h}{6}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "h=6\\sin70^\\circ=5.638\\ldots=5.64\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The height is less than the 6 m ladder, as every shorter side must be. Tangent would not work here, because the adjacent side is not known."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-example-angle",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: calculate an angle with inverse cosine",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 7 m ladder leans against a vertical wall. Its foot is 2 m from the wall on horizontal ground. Find the angle θ between the ladder and the ground, correct to 1 decimal place."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g4-lesson-03-cosine"
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relative to θ, the 2 m distance along the ground is adjacent and the 7 m ladder is the hypotenuse, so use cosine."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\cos\\theta=\\frac{2}{7}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\theta=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)=73.398\\ldots^\\circ=73.4^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Check in degree mode: cos 73.4° ≈ 0.286, and 2 ÷ 7 ≈ 0.286. The angle is steep because the foot of the ladder is close to the wall."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-elevation",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Angles of elevation and depression",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An angle of elevation is measured upward from a horizontal line; an angle of depression is measured downward from a horizontal line. The horizontal lines through the two points are parallel, so the angle of elevation from the lower point equals the angle of depression from the higher point: they are alternate angles. Add observer or instrument height only after calculating the vertical difference from eye level."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-precision",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Calculator and precision checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use degree mode for school geometry. Keep full calculator values through intermediate steps and round the final answer to the requested decimal places or significant figures. An acute angle must lie between 0° and 90°."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Check the direction of change: a larger angle with the same adjacent length gives a greater opposite height. The hypotenuse must remain longer than either shorter side."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-recall",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a right-angled triangle, the hypotenuse is 10 cm and one acute angle θ is 30°. Enter the length of the side opposite θ, in cm."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "5",
        "canonical_latex": "5",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The hypotenuse is known and the opposite side is needed, so use sine: opposite = 10 sin 30° = 10 × 0.5 = 5 cm."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-43-recall-cosine",
      "position": 9,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding: choose the ratio",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a right-angled triangle, the hypotenuse is 14 cm and one acute angle θ is 60°. Enter the length of the side adjacent to θ, in cm."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "7",
        "canonical_latex": "7",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The hypotenuse is known and the side next to θ is needed, so use cosine: adjacent = 14 cos 60° = 14 × 0.5 = 7 cm. Sine would give the opposite side instead."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g4-lesson-03-summary",
      "position": 10,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mark the right angle and reference angle, label O, A and H, then choose the ratio that contains the known and required quantities."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use inverse trigonometry for an angle, work in degree mode, delay rounding, include observer height when needed and check the result against the diagram."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
